    {
      "claim": "The captured militia member does not qualify as a prisoner of war under Article 4(2) of the Third Geneva Convention because the militia lacks the required fixed distinctive sign.",
      "reasoning": "Article 4(2) of the Third Geneva Convention lists four cumulative conditions that a militia must satisfy to be entitled to POW status. The militia meets condition (a) – it is commanded by a responsible commander – and condition (c) – its members carry arms openly – and condition (d) – it conducts operations in accordance with the laws and customs of war. However, the militia deliberately wears ordinary civilian clothing with no insignia, thereby failing condition (b), which requires \"a fixed distinctive sign recognizable at a distance.\" Because this condition is mandatory, the captured individual does not fall within the category of persons entitled to POW status under Article 4(2).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iii",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "that of having a fixed distinctive sign recognizable at a distance;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Regardless of the failure to meet Article 4(2), the detainee must be treated as a prisoner of war until a competent tribunal determines otherwise, pursuant to Article 45(1) of Additional Protocol I.",
      "reasoning": "Article 45(1) of Additional Protocol I establishes a presumption in favour of POW status for any person who takes part in hostilities and falls into the power of an adverse Party, until a competent tribunal decides the issue. The militia member actively participated in an ambush, was captured while still bearing arms, and therefore triggers this presumption. Consequently, Ardovia is obliged to afford him POW protections pending a formal status determination, even though the militia does not satisfy the full criteria of Article 4(2).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "45(1)",
          "quoted_text": "A person who takes part in hostilities and falls into the power of an adverse Party shall be presumed to be a prisoner of war, and therefore shall be protected by the Third Convention, if he claims the status of prisoner of war, or if he appears to be entitled to such status, or if the Party on which he depends claims such status on his behalf by notification to the detaining Power or to the Protecting Power. Should any doubt arise as to whether any such person is entitled to the status of prisoner of war, he shall continue to have such status and, therefore, to be protected by the Third Convention and this Protocol until such time as his status has been determined by a competent tribunal."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

